<!-- PSO-1 (SVD), in Soviet thousandths (ths = 1/6000 circle = 1.0472 mrad = 3.6 MOA),
     which is what the scope is graduated in - one windage mark and one drum click of
     0.5 ths. A 5 cm/100 m click is 0.4775 ths, 4.5% under half a thousandth.
     The main chevron is the aiming mark for whatever range the elevation drum is dialled
     to (100..1000 m). The three chevrons below it are long-range holds used ONLY with the
     drum set to 10 (1000 m): they are then 1100, 1200 and 1300 m.
     Dialling to 1000 m is 30 vertical clicks (30 x 0.5 ths = 15 ths) up from the 100 m
     setting. To read the chevrons as 1100/1200/1300 in the app, either set the zero to
     1000 m, or keep the 100 m zero and enter 30 V-clicks on the Parameters tab with the
     sight's vertical click set to 0.5 ths on the Rifle tab.
     Note the drum was cut to the issue firing tables: this model puts the 1000 m come-up
     at 14.0 ths (28 clicks) rather than 15, about 1 ths less. That affects where the main
     chevron lands, not the spacing of the three below it.
     Those three come from a trajectory: 57-N-323S LPS ball 9.6 g, BC 0.400 G1, 830 m/s,
     65 mm sight height, 1:320 mm RH, ICAO sea level - hold(R) minus hold(1000 m).
     Lower left is the stadiametric rangefinder: put the target's feet on the horizontal
     base line and its head on the curve; the numbers are hundreds of metres, 2 nearest
     the centre and 10 at the far left. Heights are the true 1.7 m subtension.
     Layout follows the canonical PSO-1 drawing; the circle is the drawn field boundary,
     not an etched element: it is sized to enclose the etching, while the real 6 deg field is
     50 ths in radius. -->
<reticle name="PSO-1 (LPS ball, thousandths)" size-x="79ths" size-y="79ths" zero-x="39.5ths" zero-y="39.5ths">
  <elements>
    <reticle-circle center-x="0ths" center-y="0ths" radius="38.5ths" fill="false" line-width="0.2ths" color="black" />
